## 1. Executive Summary

IES (Illuminating Engineering Society) photometric profiles describe the real-world angular intensity distribution of light fixtures. Architectural visualization, product rendering, and physically-based lighting all depend on IES data to produce realistic illumination from manufacturer-specified luminaires.

This PRD defines the complete scope of work required to add IES light profile support to our Godot-based RTX path tracing engine, modeled after the proven Blender Cycles implementation while adapted to our Vulkan raytracing architecture.

---

## 2. Problem Statement

Our engine currently treats all lights as **isotropic emitters** — point/omni, spot, directional, and area lights emit uniformly within their cone or solid angle. There is no mechanism to modulate intensity by direction using real-world photometric data. This limits the engine's usefulness for:

- Architectural visualization (interior/exterior lighting design)
- Product rendering with manufacturer-accurate luminaires
- Film/VFX lighting pipelines that rely on IES profiles
- Any scene requiring physically-measured light distribution

---

## 3. Goals

| # | Goal | Success Criteria |
|---|------|-----------------|
| G1 | **IES file parsing** | Parse standard IESNA LM-63 files (all photometric types A, B, C) with candela-to-watts conversion |
| G2 | **External mode** | Load `.ies` files from filesystem paths with relative path support |
| G3 | **Internal mode** | Embed IES text data inside scene resources for portability |
| G4 | **GPU upload** | Pack IES profiles into a flat GPU buffer with offset-table addressing |
| G5 | **Raytracing evaluation** | Bicubic spherical interpolation in the RT lighting shader |
| G6 | **Forward/rasterizer evaluation** | IES lookup in the forward clustered lighting pass |
| G7 | **Editor integration** | Inspector UI for IES mode toggle, file picker, and preview |
| G8 | **Deduplication** | Hash-based dedup + reference counting (same as Cycles `LightManager`) |
| G9 | **GDScript API** | Full scripting interface for IES assignment, mode switching, and strength |

