## 9. Key Technical Decisions

### 9.1 Coordinate System Adaptation

| Aspect | Blender Cycles (Z-up) | Our Engine (Y-up) |
|--------|----------------------|-------------------|
| Vertical angle | `acos(-z)` | `acos(-y)` |
| Horizontal angle | `atan(x, y)` | `atan(x, z)` |
| Pole direction | +Z = north pole | +Y = north pole |

### 9.2 RT_LightData Size Change

> [!WARNING]
> Expanding `RT_LightData` from 80 to 96 bytes is a **breaking change** for any serialized/cached shader data. Both the C++ struct and the GLSL struct must match exactly. The `static_assert` ensures compile-time validation.

**Alternative considered**: Pack `ies_slot` into an existing padding field. Rejected because:
- The current 80-byte struct has no unused padding suitable for two new fields
- A clean 16-byte expansion maintains alignment and avoids fragile bit-packing

### 9.3 IES Buffer as Separate SSBO vs. Texture

| Approach | Pros | Cons |
|----------|------|------|
| **SSBO (chosen)** | Exact Cycles parity, arbitrary size, simple offset addressing, no format conversion | Slightly more complex descriptor management |
| 1D Texture | Hardware filtering | Size limits, requires format conversion, no int-as-float trick |

### 9.4 IES Applicability

IES profiles will be supported on these light types:

| Light Type | IES Support | Rationale |
|------------|-------------|-----------|
| **OmniLight3D** | ✅ Yes | Primary use case — omnidirectional luminaire |
| **SpotLight3D** | ✅ Yes | Spot + IES = focused luminaire with angular detail |
| **DirectionalLight3D** | ❌ No | IES doesn't make physical sense for infinite-distance lights |
| **AreaLight3D** | ❌ No | Area lights already have complex emission geometry |

