## 10. Open Questions

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Q1: RT_LightData struct size**  
> Expanding RT_LightData from 80→96 bytes increases GPU memory per light by 20%. With `RT_LIGHTS_MAX = 64`, total light buffer goes from 5120 to 6144 bytes — negligible. But the GLSL struct change requires regenerating all shader `.gen.h` files. Is this acceptable, or should we try to pack IES into existing padding?

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Q2: Light-local vs. world-space IES lookup**  
> Blender Cycles evaluates IES in the light's local coordinate frame (the incoming direction vector is already in light space). Our engine currently passes world-space positions. Two options:
> - **A)** Transform hit-to-light direction into light-local space in the shader (requires adding the light's inverse rotation to RT_LightData — another 36 bytes or a quaternion at 16 bytes)
> - **B)** Evaluate in world space, requiring the user to orient the Light3D node to match the IES profile's intended orientation
>
> **Recommendation**: Option B for V1 (simpler, matches user expectation that rotating a light rotates its IES distribution). We can add Option A as a "lock orientation" toggle in V2.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Q3: Forward/rasterizer priority**  
> Should IES support in the forward clustered pass be included in V1, or deferred to V2? The raytracing path is the primary use case, but users may switch between renderers.

